WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.620
Welcome back to the David Pakman show. It's
great to welcome to program today, Neil Erwin,

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:06.859
who is the author of how to win in a winner
take all world and new book about navigating

00:00:06.859 --> 00:00:11.440
a successful career in the modern economy.
He's also a senior economic correspondent

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.480
at the New York Times. Neil, so great to have
you on today.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:15.930
Thanks for having me, David.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:21.880
So I think that in the last six to 12 months
of the political cycle, increasingly discussions

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:27.949
about work are related to number one coming,
technological unemployment and what jobs that

00:00:27.949 --> 00:00:32.870
will destroy versus what jobs it will create.
Questions of minimum wage, some broader questions

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:37.440
about the relationships of humans who work
humans to work. But let's step back from that

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.480
a little bit. I mean, if we look at the last
50 years and the idea of working for a company

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.231
for an entire career pensions, which are increasingly
not available to more and more workers, how

00:00:48.231 --> 00:00:53.270
is the landscape kind of changed about the
relationship of people to, to their jobs in

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:54.429
the United States?

00:00:54.429 --> 00:00:58.019
Yeah. I think what it means to have a job
has really shifted underneath our feet over

00:00:58.019 --> 00:01:03.149
the last, uh, last 30, 40 years as you say.
Uh, and I think the important thing is to,

00:01:03.149 --> 00:01:08.040
is to understand those changes and understand
what that implies for, for how you navigate

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.790
your own career. So what I did with this book
is I tried to go around to these big companies

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:15.890
that are dominant in the modern economy and
asked, okay, what does it take to succeed

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:19.470
and thrive in this organization? And then
what does it take to have a long and durable

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:22.549
career? Uh, we can talk more about some of
the, some of the lessons I learned, but I

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:27.020
think you're, you're fundamentally right.
I think when in a world where, uh, you know,

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.320
employers are not going to be loyal to you
necessarily, you can't count on that job,

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.170
you have being the same job five or 10 years
from now. I think the important thing is having

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:37.900
a kind of reciprocity and having a sense of
honesty that, uh, you know, a career is a

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:42.159
series of changes, a series of steps. Uh,
it's more like a series of hookups than it

00:01:42.159 --> 00:01:46.140
is like a long time, a long term marriage.
Uh, and I think that's the most important

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:50.460
lesson about how this economic change, uh,
what that implies for the rest of us.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:56.090
So there are some who will hear that, a sort
of general overview and say, well, that there's

00:01:56.090 --> 00:02:01.560
nothing natural or necessary about that change
in the sense that if only there was x type

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:07.890
of regulation that may or may be that that
change may have been restricted. For example,

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:12.540
there are others who will say that those changes
are more, uh, about life expectancy and the

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:17.460
fact that people may just by their nature
live in more places and work different jobs.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:21.870
And that's a good thing. And companies are
just sort of reacting to those desires and

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:27.150
there's really not a problem per se to be
fixed here. How much of this do you think

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:32.720
is regulation related? How much is socio cultural
change in the states? How much is something

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:33.730
else altogether?

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:38.540
Sorry. I think there's two pieces of it as
you suggest. And, and part of this is, uh,

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:43.200
choices we've made as in through the political
system and, and as a society that don't have

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.500
to be made this way, right? Uh, you know,
unions have become less powerful. Uh, there's

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:51.970
been kind of a shift, you know, the entire
Gig economy rests on the legal interpretation

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:55.780
of independent contractor law and, and you
know, companies are able to kind of outsource

00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:00.349
things because the law allows them to, um,
and you know, are choices that that could

00:03:00.349 --> 00:03:04.470
be made in Washington that would tilt the
other direction. And that's kind of what this

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:08.410
next presidential election is going to be
all about is what, what should those, uh,

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:12.300
what should those settings look like? Um,
but I think, I argue and I really strongly

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:16.849
believe that part of this is driven by technological
fundamentals and, and things that aren't really

00:03:16.849 --> 00:03:19.549
going to change no matter what happens in
the political system.

00:03:19.549 --> 00:03:23.810
So things around, uh, rapidly changing business
models. For example, I work in the newspaper

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:26.991
business, right? Um, you know, there was nothing
that could have, that government could have

00:03:26.991 --> 00:03:31.390
done that would result in, uh, the print newspaper
monopoly monopoly is still existing the way

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:35.470
they did 20 years ago when I started my career.
Uh, W it's digital media and, and that has

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:39.310
an entirely different way of working and,
and requires different types of skills than

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:43.439
the old, a more linear kind of print model
did. Um, you know, in every industry you see

00:03:43.439 --> 00:03:47.549
changes like that. Retail, finance, uh, you
know, certainly any element of technology.

00:03:47.549 --> 00:03:51.980
And I think we'd be foolish if we don't acknowledge
that those changes are driven by really powerful

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:55.290
fundamental forces and learn how to adapt
and respond accordingly.

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:59.630
Yeah, I mean, so thinking about those forces
in, in preparation for our interview, I read

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:05.430
some opinion pieces which suggested that actually,
uh, there's certainly going to be significantly

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:10.671
different outcomes in one's career based on
the industry in which they work. I mean, there

00:04:10.671 --> 00:04:16.079
are industries that go under or significantly
reduced in size and that in those cases there

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:20.540
may be factors bigger than one's own sort
of education and, and kind of actions and

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:27.860
personality, but that in many cases it's not
so much about particular skills, but it's

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:33.660
more about personality and I guess you would
call it social skills, understanding such

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:38.720
basic concepts as it's really important for
your boss to like you because if your boss

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:42.430
doesn't like you in corporate culture, it's
going to be extraordinarily difficult to get

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:45.820
ahead no matter how good you are at what you're
doing and sort of these other social dynamics.

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:50.240
And I find that really interesting, but I
also find it dangerous because there are people

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:57.230
who take that approach who like to use it
to defend drastically unequal economic outcomes.

00:04:57.230 --> 00:05:02.120
So while I find it interesting and probably
partially accurate, I don't want to go overboard

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:08.280
in that direction. I'm curious what your thought
would be on the skills, education, personality

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.980
factors in terms of dictating or, or predicting
success.

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:16.540
Yeah, so I'm, so I have a concept. I kind
of developed in this book of a, of something

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:20.850
called a glue person. And what we see in these,
the big technologically sophisticated companies

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:24.820
that are the superstar firms, they're the
real winners in the winner take all economy.

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:29.750
Um, they have team based work and pulled together
people on teams with very different technical

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:33.670
skills. So, you know, you might have a software
engineer working with a, you know, a finance

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:38.850
person working with a, you know, user interface
person, uh, working with a strategy person

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:43.260
and you need all of those skills to come together
to create a great product. And so the people

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:47.000
who are really effective are those who can
make a team more than the sum of its parts,

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:50.960
who can help that team stick together. That's
why we call them a glue person. And, um, I

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:55.550
think cultivating your ability to be that
person is really important. And, and it to

00:05:55.550 --> 00:05:59.040
the personality piece, it doesn't come naturally
to everybody. It is something we just have

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:03.410
to work on. Um, but learning how to communicate
and collaborate with people who have a different

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:07.380
kind of technical background than yourself
is just a really, really important, uh, thing.

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:12.300
And I think is, is really a crucial thing
to being the kind of worker who's really valuable

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:16.460
and needed in these types of organizations
that, uh, that are really the future and the

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:17.940
present of the, of the economy

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:23.960
when it comes to technological automation.
And there has been so much written about the

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:29.210
degree to which this is going to be a problem.
And specifically in which industries or types

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:34.700
of industries, is this going to be a problem?
How much is it being discussed now within

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:39.580
the corporations you interviewed people and
talk to for your book?

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:43.890
Yeah, I think it's the, it's the predominant,
a shift that's going on. And I think the real

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:48.921
question is, um, you know, is automation,
AI, machine learning, all these things, all

00:06:48.921 --> 00:06:53.190
these technologies that are, uh, potentially
enabling lots of different jobs to go away.

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:58.460
Uh, I think for most of us, the thing to do
is ask, okay, how can I be the driver of that

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:02.590
change? The person who is creating those algorithms,
creating those new ways of working and not

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:07.040
a victim of it. And, um, you know, it's a,
it's a scary thing to think that, you know,

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:11.080
a computer might be able to do my job in 10
years. Um, but if I'm the one trying to figure

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:15.270
out how to create the algorithms, create the
software that can do that job, uh, I can have

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:20.921
a long and viable career. Uh, it's, it's certainly
scary, but I think, uh, you know, there's

00:07:20.921 --> 00:07:24.400
always been productivity. There's always been
technological change. Uh, the question is,

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:26.401
can you become the driver of it rather than
the victim?

00:07:26.401 --> 00:07:30.130
Yeah. I'm, I'm currently in the middle of
reading the book life 3.0 by Max Tegmark,

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:33.920
which is mostly about artificial intelligence,
but it does address issues of, of technological

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:38.690
automation more broadly. And one idea he had,
and it may have been him quoting someone else,

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:44.220
I don't remember off hand, was the idea that
the jobs that are going to persist and remain

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:50.060
through automation are likely to be the ones
that operate in subjective spaces rather than

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:56.290
objective spaces, jobs where for example,
personality does play an important role or

00:07:56.290 --> 00:08:02.880
jobs that are more related to a, um, uh, subjective
advice rather than objective analysis of data,

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:08.050
so to speak, that type of thing. Do you have
any strong feelings one way or the other?

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:12.760
If we were to start trying to categorize now
the types of jobs that will and will not be

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:13.930
automated away?

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:17.692
Well, I think we can definitely start doing
that. And I think that framework that you

00:08:17.692 --> 00:08:21.700
just laid out is very much accurate. Um, so
I, you know, I have some examples in the book.

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:26.960
So if, uh, you know, in medicine for example,
uh, if you're a radiologist who looks at,

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:30.660
uh, you know, [inaudible] all day and tries
to identify things in the x rays, that's the

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:35.669
kind of thing that really can be automated
and increasingly is, uh, you know, an area

00:08:35.669 --> 00:08:38.919
where software can be really effective. If
you're an emergency room doctor, uh, that's

00:08:38.919 --> 00:08:43.950
never going to be automated. That's very much
a human touch. A very requires great flexibility,

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:48.050
a great kind of quick intelligence to see
what's wrong with somebody. Um, you know,

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:52.560
it's, that's not going to be automated in
our lifetimes. Um, in, in the law and legal

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:55.650
business, right? If you're reviewing contracts
all day, if you're a lawyer, just reviewing

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:58.630
contracts for, um, you know, possible problems.

00:08:58.630 --> 00:09:01.231
That's the kind of thing that software is
getting better and better at. Uh, if you're

00:09:01.231 --> 00:09:04.250
a courtroom lawyer, if you're a trial lawyer,
well as long as we have the legal system we

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:08.370
do now, that's something that, that's still
going to be viable. Um, so I think, uh, you

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:11.610
know, there's basically two choices. One was
what I was saying a moment ago about being

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:15.710
the driver of technological change in your
industry, being the radiologist who helps

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:19.850
make that software work. Uh, the other approach
is, uh, is put, choose one of those jobs,

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:23.560
choose one of those paths that's going to
be very hard for computers to catch up to

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:24.560
humans.

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:28.140
Is this something that should already be figuring
into the majors that students in college are

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:30.020
selecting?

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:33.510
To me it's less about what your major is in
college. It's more about what you do while

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:37.661
you're in college. Um, I think the, the, the
key thing I've learned over and over is the

00:09:37.661 --> 00:09:42.400
importance of adaptability and the, uh, cultivating
an ability to try different things, stretch

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:46.880
outside your comfort zone. And I truly come
to believe that adaptability is not just a

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:50.560
trait you're either born with or not. It's
something that you can cultivate in yourself.

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:54.230
And the way you do it is by pushing yourself
in new directions. And the best time to do

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:58.970
that is when you're either in college, Grad
School, early career jobs, where the stakes

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:02.680
are relatively low. And if you, you know,
fall on your face, it's not as big a deal.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:07.280
So what that means is whatever your core major
or your core subject to you know, uh, is,

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:09.670
uh, stretch yourself into some areas completely
different from that.

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:14.260
So if you're a, you know, if you're a w literature,
English kind of words person, uh, you know,

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:17.900
take a couple of hard computer science classes,
try language that doesn't come naturally to

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:22.040
you. If you're more of a math person, a numbers
person, you know, maybe right for the student

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:26.670
paper or you know, go out for a, for a play
in college, do something that's uh, pushing

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:30.890
yourself in new ways and keep doing that over
and over. You keep doing that in your teens

00:10:30.890 --> 00:10:34.130
and twenties, it'll become a lot easier to
do it in your forties and fifties when you're

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:35.130
in your career.

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:40.090
Uh, I believe that eventually, I don't know
if it's in five years, 25 years or, or 50

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:44.890
or a hundred years, but eventually there's
going to have to be some sort of serious reexamination

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:50.690
of just the relationship of humans to the
concept of working a full time job, uh, either

00:10:50.690 --> 00:10:57.650
for hourly wages or salary in order to be
able to achieve a sort of a viable, uh, life

00:10:57.650 --> 00:11:01.450
in the west, so to speak. We can talk about
it more worldwide, but in the west, maybe

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:06.470
we can focus our conversation. Do you agree
that that reexamination is going to have to

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:11.010
take place? If so, when do you think it's
going to have to happen? And, but maybe most

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:17.910
interestingly, are any big corporations, um,
thinking about that at this point in time.

00:11:17.910 --> 00:11:21.600
So I do think we have to kind of reopen these
questions. Um, you know, famously, uh, back

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:26.640
in the 1930s, John Maynard Keynes stopped
it. By this time we'd only be working three

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:30.951
hour work weeks or something like that. Uh,
it didn't quite go that direction. Um, so

00:11:30.951 --> 00:11:35.440
I, I, in the last chapter of the book, I tried
to kind of reflect on what I had learned about,

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:40.471
um, about having a thriving good career. And
I did some math. And the math is this. So

00:11:40.471 --> 00:11:46.200
if you start working at age 22 after college,
if you retire at 65, if you sleep eight hours

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:49.630
a night, you do the math works out to about
a quarter million hours you have to work with

00:11:49.630 --> 00:11:54.400
over the course of you're kind of prime working
years. Uh, you, those are your waking hours.

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:58.360
And, and look, that's the hardest constraint
of the mall. And we all have trade offs too,

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:05.130
to engage in between our jobs, our, uh, hobbies
and pursuits and our families and, and kind

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:06.290
of home life.

00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:10.750
And I don't, I'm not trying to be prescriptive
about how to juggle those tradeoffs. Um, I,

00:12:10.750 --> 00:12:15.200
you know, I titled This Book How to win, not
how to become a billionaire or how to become

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:20.260
a CEO of a fortune 500 company because those
paths aren't for everybody. And I think there

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:24.480
can be a really rewarding life to be had,
um, that that does treat work differently

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:28.350
than that's kind of always on. You know, we've
got to got to put in 80 hours of the, the

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:32.360
office kind of culture. Um, and I think the
important thing is being deliberate and thoughtful

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:38.380
about what you find rewarding where your ambitions
go and um, and reacting accordingly. I think

00:12:38.380 --> 00:12:42.260
smart companies are realizing that, you know,
there can be a role for people who only do

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:46.820
a part time schedule, who are willing to sacrifice
a little income to, you know, have a more,

00:12:46.820 --> 00:12:51.810
uh, you know, 20, 30 hours a week instead
of 40 or 50. Um, but, uh, you know, I think

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:55.210
the, I think as time goes on, I think more
companies are going to realize that they need

00:12:55.210 --> 00:13:01.190
that ability to, to, to adapt with people's
desires and, and, uh, kind of Worklife, uh,

00:13:01.190 --> 00:13:02.190
tradeoffs.

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:06.470
Are there specific decisions you've made in
your life that most people wouldn't necessarily

00:13:06.470 --> 00:13:10.940
think to because you work in this space and
research this, this topic?

00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:17.320
Yeah, I think the, uh, pardon me. I think
the biggest thing that I've really kind of

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:21.660
internalized, uh, is, is an idea that, uh,
is actually from Reed Hoffman who was the

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:25.680
founder of Linkedin. Yeah. He calls it tours
of duty. And that's a concept that's existed

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:30.051
in the military and in the diplomatic world
for a long time. But in the corporate world,

00:13:30.051 --> 00:13:34.300
the idea is after you spend a certain amount
of time doing a project, building something,

00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:38.210
a whatever you were hired to do, uh, then
you have a real discussion with your employer

00:13:38.210 --> 00:13:42.150
and is there a way to stretch yourself in
some new direction? Take on some new projects.

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:46.530
I'm kind of breaking up your career into,
into units. So I, I call it the three year

00:13:46.530 --> 00:13:50.940
itch. Uh, first year on a new job, you're
just trying to, you know, get up to speed.

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:53.980
Second Year you're hitting your stride, you're
getting good at that job. Then the third year

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:57.279
rolls around, it's time to start thinking
about what's the next thing and can I stretch

00:13:57.279 --> 00:14:01.550
myself in some new direction? Uh, it's doing
that over and over. That helps. On that adaptability

00:14:01.550 --> 00:14:05.920
theme we were talking about a minute ago.
Um, in my own career, I've done that, um,

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:09.940
partly by changing jobs with few times, but
also by doing something like this book. And

00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:13.710
for me, you know, I had been at the New York
Times for about three years when I kind of

00:14:13.710 --> 00:14:17.260
looked around and said, okay, what, how am
I gonna push myself, stretch myself in a new

00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:20.230
direction? And this book was a, came out of
that

00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:24.100
super interesting book. We've been talking
with Neil Erwin, who is the author of how

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:28.680
to win in a winner take all world. The book
